<p><a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/04/im-switching-back-from-spotlight-at-least-for-now/">Jason Snell gave the new Spotlight a real shot.</a> When he reviewed macOS Tahoe last September, he stopped using <a href="https://obdev.at/products/launchbar/index.html">LaunchBar</a> and stuck with Spotlight for months. He liked it. Then the betas got worse.</p>

<p>Spotlight got progressively slower for him. It missed entire categories of items, like Safari favorites. The new &#8220;Quick Keys&#8221; text shortcuts only work for Actions, which Jason called &#8220;completely beyond me.&#8221; In the linked post, Jason surrenders and goes back to LaunchBar.</p>

<p>I am really pleased to see Apple improving Spotlight annually, and I hope they continue to do so. For the vast majority of users, they are not going to buy a third-party application like LaunchBar, <a href="https://www.alfredapp.com/">Alfred</a>, or <a href="https://www.raycast.com/">Raycast</a>.</p>

<p>That said, I don&#8217;t see myself abandoning Alfred anytime soon. The dedicated launchers still (and always will) do things Spotlight doesn&#8217;t.</p><p>The post <a href="https://www.macsparky.com/blog/2026/04/why-the-dedicated-launcher-still-wins/">Why the Dedicated Launcher Still Wins</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.macsparky.com">MacSparky</a>.</p>
